  "winner_texts": {
    "4": "I would recommend that my partner keep her last name after our marriage. Here's why:\n\n1. **Personal identity**: As my partner pointed out, changing her last name could make her feel like\nshe's losing a part of herself or her individual identity. I believe it's essential to respect and\nprioritize her feelings in this matter.\n2. **Equality and autonomy**: Marriage is about building a life together, not erasing one's sense of\nself or surrendering individuality. Allowing my partner to maintain her own last name is a way to\nacknowledge our partnership as an equal union, rather than a hierarchical arrangement where she\ntakes on the more traditional role.\n3. **Modern and practical**: In today's world, changing one's last name after marriage is no longer\nnecessary or even expected. Many countries and states recognize dual surnames (e.g., Smith-Jones) or\nallow individuals to keep their original names without penalty.\n4. **Family dynamics**: While my traditional family may be upset if my partner doesn't take my name,\nI believe this is an opportunity for them to learn about and appreciate the value of equality in our\nrelationship. It's essential to communicate openly with our families and set boundaries or\nexpectations that align with our values as a couple.\n5. **Potential consequences**: Insisting my partner change her last name could lead to resentment,\ntension, and even strain on our relationship in the long run.\n\nUltimately, I would prioritize building a strong, equal partnership over appeasing traditional\nfamily expectations. By respecting my partner's decision to keep her last name, we can establish a\nfoundation for mutual respect, trust, and communication that will serve us well throughout our\nmarriage.\n\nIn this discussion, it's essential to communicate openly with each other, be empathetic, and\nconsider the long-term implications of our decisions. With honesty, understanding, and compromise,\nwe can navigate even the most sensitive topics \u2013 including last names!"
  },
